## PHASE 6 — Write the schema knowledge file (what the LLM will actually use)

**Goal:** one card per table that tells a future model how to query it and how to join.
Build it from `02_columns.md` (columns) and `03_relations.md` (joins).

**Output template — one card per table in `schema/05_knowledge.md`**
```markdown
## ORDERS
Purpose: sales order headers (one row per order).
Primary key: ORDER_ID
Key columns (with business meaning, from the COMMENTS in 02_columns.md):
- ORDER_ID — surrogate primary key of the order
- CUSTOMER_ID — the customer who placed the order (join key to CUSTOMER)
- ORDER_DATE — date the order was created
- STATUS — order lifecycle status (e.g. NEW/PAID/SHIPPED)
- TOTAL_AMOUNT — order gross total in PLN
Full column list: see 02_columns.md#orders

Relationships:
- ORDERS.CUSTOMER_ID -> CUSTOMER.CUSTOMER_ID  (many-to-one, CONFIRMED)
    join: FROM ORDERS o JOIN CUSTOMER c ON o.CUSTOMER_ID = c.CUSTOMER_ID
- ORDERS.SHIP_CITY_CODE -> CITY.CODE          (many-to-one, LIKELY)
    join: FROM ORDERS o JOIN CITY ci ON o.SHIP_CITY_CODE = ci.CODE

Common query patterns:
- Orders of one customer:
    SELECT o.ORDER_ID, o.ORDER_DATE, o.TOTAL_AMOUNT
    FROM ORDERS o WHERE o.CUSTOMER_ID = :cust_id
- Orders with customer name (join):
    SELECT o.ORDER_ID, c.NAME, o.TOTAL_AMOUNT
    FROM ORDERS o JOIN CUSTOMER c ON o.CUSTOMER_ID = c.CUSTOMER_ID
```

**Done-check:** every FOUND table has a card. This file is the deliverable that teaches
the schema — keep it up to date if tables change.

